dimension TreeSitterChange = grammar | scanner | both | none
enum TreeSitterParser = regenerate | keep
enum ZedExtensionPin = update | keep
enum RequiredChange = modify | keep
enum VerificationResult = unverified | passed | failed

## The new language is redesigned from agreed syntax and Lean semantics.
## This model concerns lean/ and packages/next and their future consumers.
## Every component below belongs to the redesigned language, including its tools.
## No edge asks for changes to the prototype or migration between implementations.
## Each has node identifies a responsibility to review, not a mandatory edit.
## Repeated consumers identify different upstream causes, not repeated work.
## This is the target change flow; a node does not assert an implementation exists.
## Implementation and verification evidence belong in separate records.
type RedesignDxnLanguage needs TreeSitterChange {
  has modify-lean-specification: ModifyLeanSpecification
}

## Record written syntax and examples in lean/notes. Review both syntax implementations
## and their agreement. TreeSitterChange describes the required Tree-sitter edit;
## a semantic-only change can keep the existing syntax and use none.
type ModifySyntaxContract needs TreeSitterChange {
  has modify-core-parser: ModifyCoreParser
  has modify-tree-sitter-package: ModifyTreeSitterPackage
}

## Specify the meaning and validity of parsed declarations in Lean definitions and proofs.
## Every redesign decision starts here, including syntax decisions in lean/notes.
## A syntax-only change may retain the semantic definitions and proofs.
## Semantic implementation must agree with this specification.
type ModifyLeanSpecification needs TreeSitterChange {
  has verify-lean-specification: VerifyLeanSpecification
  has modify-syntax-contract: ModifySyntaxContract
  has modify-semantic-analyzer: ModifySemanticAnalyzer
  has modify-refactorings: ModifyRefactorings
}

## Build the specification and check its proofs and concrete examples with make lean.
## These proofs establish properties of Lean definitions, not of the TypeScript implementation.
type VerifyLeanSpecification { }

## Review the grammar, scanner and their consumers. Regenerate parser sources
## when the grammar changes; scanner-only changes keep those generated sources.
## Supporting source paths and integration details are in the architecture README.
type ModifyTreeSitterPackage needs TreeSitterChange {
  state tree-sitter-parser: TreeSitterParser = when TreeSitterChange {
    grammar -> regenerate
    scanner -> keep
    both -> regenerate
    none -> keep
  }

  has check-parser-agreement: CheckParserAgreement
  has modify-zed-extension: ModifyZedExtension
  has vscode-extension-impact: VSCodeExtensionImpact
}

## Adapt syntax reading, parsed declarations and recovery. Review formatting, editor
## features and syntax diagnostics of the redesigned language. Parsed-output changes also
## inform the semantic implementation reviewed under ModifyLeanSpecification.
type ModifyCoreParser {
  has modify-formatter: ModifyFormatter
  has modify-editor-features: ModifyEditorFeatures
  has modify-refactorings: ModifyRefactorings
  has diagnostic-impact: DiagnosticImpact
}

## Compare the accepted declarations, problems and values of packages/next with Lean.
## Run make agreement on shared and generated cases, directly and through the parser.
## This tests implementation agreement; syntax recovery has its own production tests.
type CheckSpecificationAgreement { }

## Review hover, completion, navigation, symbols, naming quick fixes and semantic tokens against
## the new parser and analyzer, including documentation and incomplete source.
type ModifyEditorFeatures {
  has language-server-impact: LanguageServerImpact
}

## Refactoring is a shared language operation, not an editor-only feature. Review
## Lean preconditions, refusals and the mapping from old values to new values;
## a rename preserves validity and maps values bijectively. Review identity,
## inverse and composition laws where applicable, not for every kind of refactoring.
## Parser changes affect source ranges and edits; semantic changes affect validity
## and references. Both the CLI and language server must use the shared operation.
## Preserve unrelated source text and review the redesigned language's stored records;
## a source-rename proof alone does not establish record transformations.
type ModifyRefactorings {
  has check-refactoring-agreement: CheckRefactoringAgreement
  has stored-evidence-impact: StoredEvidenceImpact
  has cli-impact: CliImpact
  has language-server-impact: LanguageServerImpact
}

## Compare shared refactoring refusals and accepted results with executable Lean.
## Implementation tests additionally check precise edits, unchanged surrounding text,
## and re-parsing and re-checking the resulting source. CLI/LSP and manual editor
## verification remain responsibilities of the consumer branches.
type CheckRefactoringAgreement { }

## Check that Tree-sitter and the core parser agree on the changed syntax.
## The result and supporting evidence are recorded separately.
type CheckParserAgreement { }

## Review name resolution and semantic validation of the changed parser output.
## Results and diagnostics reach both the CLI and the language server.
type ModifySemanticAnalyzer {
  has modify-refactorings: ModifyRefactorings
  has check-specification-agreement: CheckSpecificationAgreement
  has modify-evaluator: ModifyEvaluator
  has diagnostic-impact: DiagnosticImpact
  has cli-impact: CliImpact
  has language-server-impact: LanguageServerImpact
}

## Review combinations, conditions and evaluation of the redesigned constructs.
## Changed meaning can affect stored evidence and the results exposed by both interfaces.
type ModifyEvaluator {
  has stored-evidence-impact: StoredEvidenceImpact
  has cli-impact: CliImpact
  has language-server-impact: LanguageServerImpact
}

## Review validation, identity and review counts for records of the redesigned language.
## Refactoring may require transforming these records or reporting their invalidation.
## Importing prototype records is outside this model.
type StoredEvidenceImpact {
  has cli-impact: CliImpact
  has language-server-impact: LanguageServerImpact
}

## Review formatting independently of semantic analysis. The formatter reads
## syntax directly and serves both interfaces.
type ModifyFormatter {
  has cli-impact: CliImpact
  has language-server-impact: LanguageServerImpact
}

## Review the shared diagnostic contract for parser and semantic-analyzer
## errors and warnings: severity, code, message and source location. Both sources
## reach the CLI and language server, including diagnostics for incomplete input.
type DiagnosticImpact {
  has cli-impact: CliImpact
  has language-server-impact: LanguageServerImpact
}

## Review command-line behavior reached through the shared language library.
## The redesigned CLI must exercise the shared packages/next behavior.
type CliImpact {
  has verify-cli: VerifyCli
}

## Exercise the affected commands through the executable, including text and JSON,
## exit codes, diagnostics, formatting and parity with language-server behavior.
## Record automated command output separately from implementation completion.
type VerifyCli { }

## Review language-server behavior and how it reaches the editor clients.
## The redesigned language server must exercise shared packages/next behavior.
type LanguageServerImpact {
  has verify-language-server: VerifyLanguageServer
  has vscode-language-client-impact: VSCodeLanguageClientImpact
  has zed-language-client-impact: ZedLanguageClientImpact
}

## Review VS Code behavior supplied by the language server.
type VSCodeLanguageClientImpact {
  has verify-vscode-client: VerifyVSCodeClient
}

## Review Zed behavior supplied by the language server, separately from its
## direct Tree-sitter grammar integration.
type ZedLanguageClientImpact {
  has verify-zed-client: VerifyZedClient
}

## Review Zed's direct grammar integration, including its pin and syntax queries.
## Grammar and scanner changes both require updating the pinned commit.
## A semantic-only change keeps the pin and reviews existing syntax behavior.
## This state describes required work, not completion.
type ModifyZedExtension needs TreeSitterChange {
  state zed-extension-pin: ZedExtensionPin = when TreeSitterChange {
    grammar -> update
    scanner -> update
    both -> update
    none -> keep
  }

  has verify-zed-extension: VerifyZedExtension
}

## Review VS Code's direct syntax support: its TextMate grammar and language
## configuration. Existing support may already handle the grammar change.
## The required-change state allows modify or keep; it is not derived from
## TreeSitterChange and does not describe implementation completion.
## Language-server behavior follows the separate language-client impact path.
type VSCodeExtensionImpact {
  state required-change: RequiredChange

  has verify-vscode-extension: VerifyVSCodeExtension
}

## Verify the language server against the redesigned runtime, including
## diagnostics, protocol and CLI parity tests,
## including changed syntax, incomplete input and source ranges in unsaved buffers.
## Actual editor behavior requires the developer checks in the client nodes.
## Record the outcome, developer and evidence separately.
type VerifyLanguageServer { }

## Required verification of affected LSP behavior in VS Code. Automated server
## and bundle tests support this; a developer must load the development extension
## and exercise the affected diagnostics, formatting or language features on
## valid and incomplete source, including unsaved edits. Record results separately.
type VerifyVSCodeClient { }

## Required verification of affected LSP behavior in Zed. Automated server/parity
## tests support this; a developer must load the development extension, confirm
## server startup and exercise the affected language features in an unsaved buffer.
## Record results separately from the direct grammar integration checks.
type VerifyZedClient { }

## Verify Zed against the redesigned grammar and runtime: grammar pin, parsing,
## query compilation and extension build checks. Prototype checks alone do not suffice.
## A developer must also open changed syntax in Zed and inspect affected syntax
## highlighting, outline, indentation, brackets or run actions. Record results
## separately; passing the automated gate does not establish these manual checks.
type VerifyZedExtension { }

## Required verification of VS Code's direct syntax support for both modify and
## keep. Run grammar and configuration checks against the redesigned syntax.
## A developer must load the development extension and inspect affected syntax
## highlighting, comment toggling, indentation or bracket behavior. Verify that
## existing support still handles the change when keep is chosen. Record the
## outcome, developer and evidence separately from this required work.
type VerifyVSCodeExtension { }

# Evidence recorded by the coding agent for an automated verification command.
# The implicit context identifies the verification responsibility and combination.
# Unverified means no decisive result has been established; the presence of a
# record alone does not establish success. No evidence also leaves work unverified.
# Revision identifies the source tested, output names the saved command log,
# and at records when the verification ran. Human editor checks remain separate.
record AutomatedVerification {
  command: NonEmptyString
  result: VerificationResult
  revision: NonEmptyString
  output: RelativePath
  at: Timestamp
}

# The coding agent records automated checks, not human editor sign-off.
actor CodingAgent records AutomatedVerification
